Morning nasal congestion and mild throat clearing secondary to domestic dust mite allergy
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Morning nasal congestion, Pale boggy turbinates, Dust exposure exacerbation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-030
Reason for Visit: Persistent severe nasal congestion and morning cough requiring assessment to rule out acute bacterial sinusitis or evolving asthma exacerbation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe nasal obstruction and a persistent cough upon waking. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to auscultate the lungs, assess the nasal mucosa, and rule out infectious rhinosinusitis or occult reactive airway disease.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I wake up every morning completely stuffed up, hacking and clearing my throat constantly.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes bilateral nasal mucosal swelling most prominent immediately upon waking. Reports frequent morning throat clearing to mobilize accumulated post-nasal drip. Notes clear mucoid rhinorrhea resolving gradually throughout the day. Describes a mild exacerbation of congestion during bedroom cleaning or vacuuming. Denies clinical fever, acute severe sinus pain, or purulent thick discharge. Mentions a transverse nasal crease derived from repetitive upward rubbing, and mild conjunctival injection paired with morning itching.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Sleep quality is impaired due to mouth breathing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/78 | HR: 80 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, mouth breathing at rest, occasionally clearing throat.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals pale boggy nasal turbinates with clear mucoid secretions. A transverse nasal crease is present. Diagnostic triad confirmed: morning nasal congestion, pale boggy turbinates, and dust exposure exacerbation. Pulmonary auscultation reveals clear bilateral lung fields without audible wheeze or prolonged expiratory phase. Absence of acute sinus tenderness to palpation. Mild conjunctival injection noted.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is carpeted with heavy drapes.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient trialing over-the-counter oral decongestants with rebound symptoms.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Morning nasal congestion and mild throat clearing secondary to domestic dust mite allergy (Allergic Rhinitis) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, lack of purulent discolored nasal discharge, and symptom resolution during the day.

Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal pulmonary auscultation, absence of wheeze, and lack of exertional dyspnea or nighttime awakenings with shortness of breath.

Upper Respiratory Infection (Viral) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Chronic, episodic nature restricted specifically to mornings and dust exposure, rather than an acute viral prodrome.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's chronic morning congestion and post-nasal drip cough required a thorough clinical examination to exclude infectious pathology or lower respiratory tract involvement. Delaying assessment carried the risk of misattributing the symptoms to an acute infectious process, which could lead to inappropriate antibiotic prescribing. The home evaluation confidently confirmed an allergic etiology with no lower airway compromise, facilitating targeted environmental and symptomatic management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for a daily intranasal corticosteroid spray (e.g., fluticasone). Recommendation for a daily non-sedating oral antihistamine.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use an allergen-impermeable encasement for mattresses and pillows, directly reducing the inhaled dust mite burden during the 8 hours of sleep when symptoms are generated.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Dust-mite proof bedding covers.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess the efficacy of the intranasal steroid.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience sudden severe shortness of breath, wheezing, or difficulty speaking in full sentences.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical allergic rhinitis; formal allergy testing can identify specific environmental triggers.
Clinical Course: Intranasal steroids require 1-2 weeks of consistent daily use to reach maximal anti-inflammatory effect.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if your nasal discharge becomes thick and bloody, if you develop a severe unremitting headache, or if you begin wheezing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You are allergic to dust mites in your bedding. The inflammation causes your nose to swell and produce clear mucus while you sleep. The steroid spray reduces the swelling, and the covers trap the dust mites.
Actionable Steps: Use the nasal spray every single day, not just when symptoms are bad. Wash bedding weekly in hot water. Avoid taking decongestant pills long-term.

